%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a0001cc40633e2242a75f7015c8a9162, type: 3}
  m_Name: Quest1_Level3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  opening: "Looking at climate can only do so much to explain ecosystem processes
    and wildfire risk. Another important factor to consider is vegetation, and how
    that couples with climate in different locations to create differences in vegetation
    cover, vegetation behavior, and vegetation response to fire.\r\n\r\nWithin FM
    there are 5 patches: high elevation, low elevation, north-facing patch, south-facing
    patch, and a riparian patch. Tap on each to explore some of the following characteristics
    while keeping note of:\r\nBiomass accumulation \r\nVegetation height \r\nProductivity
    (Net Primary Productivity) \r\nPlant Respiration \r\nPlant Transpiration \r\nEvapotranspiration\r\nVegetation
    Water Access \r\nRain accumulation \r\nTypes of vegetation and structures \r\n\r\nClick
    on the \"ready\" when ready to answer some questions in order to proceed"
  defaultOptions:
  - North
  - South
  - High
  - Low
  - Riparian
  questions:
  - prompt: This zone has the largest year-to-year variation in plants' primary productivity
      (or energy generation), above-ground biomass, respiration, and transpiration,
      characterized by an average elevation within the watershed.
    options: []
    correctIndex: 0
  - prompt: 'This zone has the lowest amount of above-ground biomass and is considered
      the least productive in terms of net primary productivity because this zone
      is mostly composed of grass.

      

      This zone receives more solar
      radiation and has the most direct sunlight of all of the zones. More sunlight
      and radiation can lead to increased temperatures, increased evaporation, and
      less water available in the soil for plants.'
    options: []
    correctIndex: 1
  - prompt: "This zone is one where the plants have access to water from a stream.\r\n\r\nThis
      zone is the only zone with coast live oaks. Coast live oaks average 10\u201315
      meters in height, compared to chaparral, where mature shrubs average 2\u20134
      meters.\r\n\r\nWater is not a limiting factor in this zone, leading to increased
      biomass and potentially taller plants."
    options: []
    correctIndex: 4
  - prompt: 'This zone has, on average, a lower above-ground biomass for the chaparral
      shrubs.

      

      In this zone, rainfall does not determine how much
      access the vegetation has to water, as water naturally travels "downslope"
      to lower-elevation patches like this one.

      

      It can also be categorized
      as the least "thirsty" for its low transpiration levels.'
    options: []
    correctIndex: 3
  - prompt: 'This zone has the most above-ground biomass in comparison to the other
      zones and consistent vegetation height, as no fire or other disturbances occur
      at this elevation level in any of our simulations.

      

      The amount
      of access the vegetation has to water is very dependent on the amount of rainfall
      this zone accumulates; however, it does accumulate the most rainfall out of
      all the zones.

      

      It also has the highest evapotranspiration
      and potentially the most ecosystem functionality.'
    options: []
    correctIndex: 2
